    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Corbin Carroll** (86.26) standing as our premier offensive anchor. Facing Zack Littell, **Corbin Carroll** benefits significantly from a favorable 4.83 xERA matchup and a massive +19% boost from the opposing SP skill profile, making him a high-leverage start for both power and our pursuit of stolen bases. He is flanked by **Josh Naylor** (78.46) and **James Wood** (75.08), both of whom are poised for production in hitter-friendly environments with projected wind support. **Brice Turang** (69.59) remains a core component of our strategy; despite the lack of an opposing starter projection, the +14% park factor and the premium #3 lineup slot ensure his high-efficiency utility is locked into the MI spot.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Travis Bazzana** (50.93), who barely missed the cut for the final outfield or utility slots. While **Travis Bazzana** carries an appealing +10% bump for his projected lead-off spot, the optimizer favored the higher raw base projections and specific platoon advantages of **Tyler Soderstrom** (73.03) and **Henry Bolte** (55.26). Elsewhere, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (56.15) holds a marginal score but remains in the lineup thanks to a +14% platoon edge, even as he faces the stiffest test of the day against Joe Ryan (3.19 xERA). We are monitoring the status of **Alex Freeland** (53.77), whose low batting order projection (#9) puts him right on the bubble; should the official lineups shift him upward, his value could see a meaningful correction.\n\nWe are operating under \"Lineup Pending\" designations for nearly the entire roster, which introduces inherent volatility. **Dalton Rushing** (56.25) and **Alex Freeland** are particularly sensitive to these shifts; if they are pushed further down the order, we may see their scores dip below our comfort threshold. Additionally, while we have kept **Mickey Moniak** on the bench due to his IL designation, we continue to carry depth pieces like **Austin Riley**, **Kyle Karros**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Max Muncy**, **Zach McKinstry**, and **Carson Williams** to pivot if late-breaking weather or lineup changes render our primary starters inefficient. The focus remains on maximizing our stolen base potential through high-upside plays like **Jose Caballero** (63.85), who carries a +13% platoon boost despite the difficult task of facing a 3.75 xERA starter.",
